Compare production designs with evidence and uncertainty about the future (@engineering-production-decision: 0, is #chapter, #hephaestus-core "Investigate-production-schedules-rework-and-inventory-time.lingua#r_00A308F2C75A43925C580DC3C9") { r_005D33EC2ADADFCFCC3E4CE8CD
The four-slot scenario can screen a concrete requirement: produce two accepted items by time four, release no more than two raw items, leave no unfinished work, and keep total item-time at most six. Capacity design (K_A,K_B)=(0,1) fails the accepted-output requirement; (1,1) has the explicit witness schedule; (1,2) also passes but does not improve the primary optimum in this scenario. Additional waiting space may have cost or other benefits, but neither is supplied by the model. Report a conditional feasible design rather than inventing a measured investment return.

The optimum knows the entire availability calendar, which jobs will need rework and which will be accepted. This information boundary matters. If job 2's final rejection is not known before processing, a real release policy cannot simply imitate the perfect-information decision to leave it untouched. A scenario optimum is a bound on what an informed scheduler could achieve. An implementable policy must map only available observations to actions. Different future scenarios sharing the same observed history must receive the same action until new information arrives.

Distinguish planning, scheduling and control. Planning may choose capacities, staffing or a release target; scheduling assigns particular jobs to particular times; control changes decisions as observations arrive. The earlier feedback block shows how measurements and actuator limits affect a dynamic policy. Here, discrete item identity and resource precedence add constraints that a scalar rate controller does not capture. A control signal requesting two departures cannot manufacture eligible jobs or destination space.

Compare uncertainty models honestly. A fixed availability trace is a scenario, a hard set of possible calendars supports a worst-case question, and a probability law supports expected or probabilistic performance. With four slots and no lower bound on availability, the all-unavailable calendar is permitted, so a guarantee of two accepted outputs is impossible. Removing that calendar requires evidence or a declared operating constraint. Optimizing each scenario separately does not by itself produce a single policy that works across all scenarios.

The study checks all 256 two-machine Boolean calendars of length four for a two-job workload and nine buffer-capacity pairs. This is exhaustive for those bounds, not a distribution of factory days. Equal weight to each calendar would be an additional probability assumption. Its monotonicity checks concern optimized output under unchanged routes and quality. If larger buffers cause longer waits that degrade quality or delay defect detection, route/acceptance assumptions themselves change; the earlier set-inclusion argument does not settle useful output in that different model.

For physical validation, define records of release identity, machine-start and finish times, route decisions, inspection evidence, accepted/scrap exits and interruptions. Reconcile machine visits with item histories and inventory snapshots. A missing record, duplicated identifier or changed clock reference can create an apparent balance failure. Use [Review reporting criticism and the limits of reproduced evidence](../../inquiry/core/Review-reporting-criticism-and-the-limits-of-reproduced-evidence.lingua#r_00938E8A05E83C0B28227241FA) to connect the actual evidence to the claimed result and to record corrections. Passing the author's enumeration checks does not establish accurate observations of a real line.

**Work:** propose a policy that cannot see future acceptance labels, state its permitted observations and compare it with the scenario optimum on two futures with the same initial history. Identify which requirement fails first if a single slot becomes unavailable. Add a resource such as a shared operator or a setup change, then revise the state and feasible-action rule before optimizing. Submit a design comparison with its resource, quality, service and evidence limits, and revise it after another reader identifies an omitted dependency.

